00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.021
Today I'm going to do an
introduction on Google Cloud

00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:43.520
Spanner.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.680
I will start off with
what Cloud Spanner is

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.280
and where it comes from.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.930
I'm going to talk about how it
works, like, behind the scenes.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.590
I'm going to give you
a small demo of how

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.670
you can get started on
Google Cloud Platform

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.460
and on Cloud Spanner.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.650
And at the end, I'm going
to talk a little bit

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.470
about some use cases and
industries and protocols

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.390
where you can use Cloud Spanner.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.890
All right, so lets get started.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.590
So what is Cloud Spanner?

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.770
Imagine the scenario is that
you have an application that

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:13.610
grows over time.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.760
It gets viral, and a lot
of people are using it.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.480
And this system is backed
by a relational database.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.400
So for instance, you
have billing data

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.670
or any kinds of things like
payments and things like that.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.520
At some point, you would
need to scale your database

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.490
to cope with the traffic
that you are getting.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.090
So you have two options.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.880
You basically look
at either scaling

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.420
up your relational database,
but this has ceiling, right?

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.910
Like, you basically have to
wait for your cloud provider

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.342
to provide a bigger machine
or for your hardware vendor

00:01:43.342 --> 00:01:45.050
to provide a bigger
machine, and then you

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.000
have to move everything over.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.480
Very, very complicated.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.240
The other option is that if you
outgrow your vertical scalable

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.090
solution, you are looking
at other solutions which

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.380
are scaling horizontal.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.680
So you are looking
usually at NoSQL databases

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.970
where you scale horizontally.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:06.950
But by scaling horizontally
and going the NoSQL way,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.570
you're giving up some
really, really nice features

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.039
about relational databases.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.310
So in between
these two, you have

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.920
something that is called
charting where you can

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.990
chart a relational database.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.540
But you give up a
lot of the qualities

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.730
as well for the
relational database.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:25.610
And it gets really,
really complicated.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.220
So we actually were faced
with exactly that problem

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.600
here in Google.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.990
Our ads business was
running on a sharded MySQL,

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.760
and we needed to
find a solution which

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.320
enables us to scale at the rate
we were scaling and growing

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.840
the company without
having interruptions,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.230
without taking down
the entire database.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:49.010
You can imagine, like,
Google is a 24/7 business.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:53.270
And we cannot afford basically
taking our system offline

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.790
for maintenance,
for schema upgrades,

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.490
or for migrating from
one system to another.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:07.400
So after we had our system
sharded for many, many years,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.470
and the last shard
took two years,

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.480
we built actually
a new system which

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.700
was giving us these
attributes that we are looking

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.300
for-- a system that
can scale horizontally,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.390
is globally distributed,
and strongly consistent.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.360
So please meet Cloud Spanner.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.490
Cloud Spanner is
actually the system

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.020
that we are using
inside of Google.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.220
So Cloud Spanner we have used
for many, many years inside

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.120
of Google.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.900
And we now make it
available as a product,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.320
as a fully-managed product,
under the Google Cloud Platform

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.040
umbrella.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.290
So just to recap,
what are the features

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.310
of Google Cloud Spanner?

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.170
It is an enterprise-grade
database.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.790
It is globally distributed.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.410
It is strongly consistent.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.580
We don't sacrifice
anything there.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.140
And you combine the benefits
of a relational database

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.532
with the horizontal scale.

00:03:56.532 --> 00:03:58.240
So you get a lot of
the features that you

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.750
are familiar with from
a traditional relational

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.090
database.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.640
So things like
ACID transactions,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.180
things like the
Structured Query Language,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.580
and you get also
an SLA from us that

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.490
is up to five nines of uptime.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:18.000
So this is about 23
seconds per month

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.399
that we allow as downtime.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.330
So many questions
that we get asked are,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.610
like, how does
Google Cloud Spanner

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.400
compare to either
traditional databases--

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.870
traditional
relational databases--

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:36.150
or to the more new or modern
ones, the nonrelational,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.460
so-called NoSQL databases?

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.280
So you here see a
table where we compare

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.030
a couple of the attributes
of these two databases.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.640
And I want to start with
relational database.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.000
As we saw in the first scenario,
like in a relational database,

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.260
you basically--

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.300
you can only scale vertically.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.660
You can add main memory, you can
add disk, and you can add CPU.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.740
But at some point, you
reach a scalability ceiling.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:05.220
And if you want to stay
relational, what you had to do

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.040
is basically shard your
relational database.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.200
So you basically
say, OK, I'm going

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.670
to have part of
this table there,

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:15.120
part of this table
on a second database.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.270
And then you pull out
all the nice features

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:22.050
from relational database,
like ACID transactions

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.850
and referential
constraints, and you

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.640
have to re-implement these
things in your application.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.150
So its a lot, a lot of things
that you have to deal with.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.760
And it's a lot of
operational overhead.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.480
So basically, if you look at
a relational database system,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.210
it's not really
scalable, in a sense.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.260
It's really difficult to scale.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.320
Now, a lot of companies,
they went like, OK,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.670
scalability is something
I cannot sacrifice on.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.670
I need a scalable database.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.830
So they went the NoSQL way.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.600
NoSQL has a lot of nice things.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.440
You have more structure
flexibility in your schemas.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.000
And you can actually
distribute it under, like,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.260
through many, many nodes
and horizontal scaling.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.370
But none of these
traditional NoSQL databases

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.100
have actually a
strong consistency

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.950
across multiple
regions and is still

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:17.710
usable in the sense of
that it has low latency.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.970
Of course, databases,
NoSQL databases,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.230
where you can tweak
the consistency model

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.510
and can decide how much
consistency you want

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.020
to have for your
queries, but still, it's

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.710
really, really complicated
and usually it gets very slow

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.930
if you want to have a
very strong consistency

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.580
for your NoSQL databases.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.780
Now, the other thing
is replication.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.340
For the traditional
database system,

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.910
you can replicate to
multiple instances

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.070
and then use these
instances, for instance,

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.450
for read-only replica, by
then freeing up your master

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:56.280
node from some read performance
that it usually uses up,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.920
and then can actually
use more rights

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.500
or do more rights
on your master node.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.610
So how does Cloud
Spanner actually work?

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.960
I want to start with a
logical architectural overview

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.274
of our Cloud Spanner.

00:07:13.274 --> 00:07:15.315
So you start with Cloud
Spanner with an instance.

00:07:15.315 --> 00:07:18.230
So an instance is basically
your resource pool,

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.080
your compute nodes,
and your storage.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.350
And in this instance,
you can create databases.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.950
So you can create, I think,
about 1,024 databases

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:31.760
and then about 2,000 tables
in each of your databases.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.257
All these databases and
tables live in this instance.

00:07:34.257 --> 00:07:35.840
So this is your
resource pool, and you

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.592
can create multiple databases.

00:07:37.592 --> 00:07:39.050
Now, this is
something that you are

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.000
familiar with from a
relational database.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.540
Now, there is another
structure which is somewhat

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.160
hidden from you, but
we talk about it,

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.930
and that is actually splits.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.960
So splits are basically
where we take a table

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.730
and order it by your
primary key alphabetically

00:07:55.730 --> 00:08:00.200
or lexicographically, and then
we take ranges off your table

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.360
and split it by ranges.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.270
And that enables this
really, really cool thing

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:06.230
in Cloud Spanner.

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.400
So what are we doing with this?

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.890
So as mentioned, you
get with an instance

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.610
a resource pool of
compute nodes and storage.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.420
Now, what we can do is
basically we replicate all this

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:19.390
in multiple zones
and by that get

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:23.390
a high availability and also
some scalability in terms

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.950
of read that we can perform.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:30.260
Now, to get high read
and write throughputs,

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.110
we actually use these
splits and distribute them

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:37.070
across the computes
that are available.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.049
And by that, we have
multiple computes

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:42.500
basically being able to
manage reads and writes.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.120
So what we have
here, you can see

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.300
as we separate the compute and
storage, we have three zones.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.470
So this is a schematic
for a regional instance.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.310
Now, in my regional instance
here, I choose three nodes.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:57.410
So I have three nodes
replicated to all three zones.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.380
So in total, you get
nine compute nodes.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:05.000
Now, what is really important
for these splits or these parts

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.740
of your table, these ranges, is
that there is exactly and never

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.970
more than one compute node
responsible for a split.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.830
And we do this by using Paxos.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.230
So we use basically,
here in this example,

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.650
I have the split one.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:22.390
I have in each zone
one compute node

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.100
who is responsible
for that split one.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.140
And then from these
three nodes that

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.060
are responsible for split
one, I need to pick a leader.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:33.200
So here in this
case, we use actually

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.660
Paxos, which is a
consensus algorithm

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.150
to choose a leader
in this group.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.310
But as I mentioned, it's
really, really important

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:43.250
that we never have two
leaders for a split.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.710
So how do we do that
actually performantly?

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.050
And that's where True
Time is coming in.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.510
So you see, True
Time is something

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.500
that we invented inside Google
to be able to have something

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.100
like a global wall clock time.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.740
So let me talk a little
bit more about True Time.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.490
So True Time is basically
what we do all over the world

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.280
globally in our data centers.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.520
We have GPS and atomic clocks.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.100
And what we need actually
is we need something

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.170
that we can trust
all around the world.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.790
So let me give you an example.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.210
Imagine you have
your friend with you,

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.270
and you compare watches, and
you synchronize these watches.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.360
And then you went off, and
you meet again after a week,

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.770
or let's say a month.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.450
Your watches will have drifted.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.270
Let's assume it's
not a smartwatch that

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:30.800
resynchronizes by itself.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.430
Let's have one of these
old analog watches, right?

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.110
So they will drift
from each other.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:40.460
And this drift is actually
dangerous in the sense of--

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:43.790
if I trust these logs, I have
actually two different times

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:44.990
in two different places.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:48.860
And by that, I could have
two leaders for a split

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.206
and get inconsistency.

00:10:50.206 --> 00:10:51.830
So we needed something
that we actually

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.960
can trust all around the world.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.060
So we invented True
Time, which gives you

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.640
actually not a specific
time, but it gives you

00:10:58.640 --> 00:10:59.690
a range of time.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:02.540
It gives you an upper
and a lower bound

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:06.110
for a time that is
either passed or not

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:07.460
passed all around the world.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.430
So let me be a little
bit more concrete here.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:12.490
So True Time
basically quantifies

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:16.920
the worst possible error or
drift between data centers.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.170
You get a lower bound.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.990
That lower bound defines
that this timestamp

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:23.060
has passed all over the world.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.500
And you get an
upper bound, which

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:28.110
is a timestamp that has
passed nowhere in the world.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:30.320
Now, with these
two timestamps, you

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.110
can actually make sure
that you have never

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:36.170
more than one leader for a
split, which is really, really

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.080
cool.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.380
So to synchronize actually
our atomic clocks,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:44.160
we use GPS time masters
in our data centers.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.850
These are synchronizing
our atomic clocks.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.010
And then these atomic
clocks, basically when

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:52.070
we get a True Time for
Spanner, but also other use

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.240
cases in the Google
Cloud Platform,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.860
we ask a subset of time
masters-- so the GPS

00:11:57.860 --> 00:11:59.870
time masters and atomic clocks.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:02.920
And then if there's any
outlier, we just say, like, OK,

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:04.740
this is a bad time master.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:05.590
We don't trust it.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:06.360
We take it out.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.700
But basically, since we have
a subset of this time master,

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.400
we basically take
the majority of that.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.570
Now, in the normal operation,
these atomic clocks

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:19.720
drift by about 50
microseconds per second or 200

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:23.050
microseconds per second,
and we resynchronize them

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.205
about every 30 seconds.

00:12:26.205 --> 00:12:27.580
Now, some of you
might ask, like,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:30.970
what happens if all our
time masters go down

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.230
in the data center?

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:35.380
Now, we can actually
ask True Time timestamp

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.750
from a different data
center, and we just

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.540
calculate in the
latency it takes

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.520
to travel to this
other data center

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.560
and get the True Time timestamp.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:48.610
What that means is the epsilons
or the range between the upper

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.770
and lower bound gets bigger.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:54.100
And by that, the database
will actually slow down,

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:55.120
but it will not stop.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.710
It will still be operational.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:03.030
So what is the data format
in Google Cloud Spanner?

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:06.150
The data format is actually
what you are very familiar with.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:09.080
We are using for Google
Cloud Spanner SQL and Z2011

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.180
with a couple of extensions.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:13.010
So you can use
just the normal DDL

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:17.450
as you are familiar with from a
traditional relational database

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.130
and create your schemas.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:22.790
So here in this example, I have
a singers table and an album

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:23.540
table.

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:26.510
And I just create
that with strong types

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:31.310
string or integer and things
like define primary keys.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:34.220
Now, there is a feature, since
this is a distributed database,

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.590
there are a couple of
things that are not

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.600
available in Google
Cloud Spanner compared

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:40.520
to a traditional
database system, things

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.710
like referential constraints.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.830
But we have something where
you can optimize your access

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:46.550
patterns.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.130
And that is something
that we call interleaving.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:53.300
So this is a technique to
basically physically interleave

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.220
child tables into parent tables.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.620
By that you gain data locality.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:14:01.160
So you can actually group
data together and make sure

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.550
that queries, if you do
joins a lot on the parent

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:07.190
table and the child table,
that these queries can

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:12.770
be answered by one node or
only very few subset of nodes.

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:14.490
All right, so how
does it look like?

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:17.570
How can you actually create
interleaved data tables?

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.540
Here, in this example,
we have our singers table

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:22.454
that we saw from
the example before,

00:14:22.454 --> 00:14:23.870
and then we have
the albums table,

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:26.360
and you just say, like,
interleaved fingers.

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:30.200
What's really important here is
that you share the primary key

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.220
of your parent table.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:34.010
That is really important so
that the interleaving actually

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:34.820
works.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.110
I want to tell you--

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.090
be a little bit
careful with this.

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:40.580
Interleaving means
that all the data

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:43.340
in your parent table and child
table end up in one split.

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:46.010
We talked about splits earlier.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.180
One split is managed
by one compute node.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.980
So if you have one row
in your parent table,

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:56.660
all the stuff, like all the
data from your interleave table

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:00.660
that qualifies for this primary
key ends up in the same split.

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.960
So if you have a
large child table

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.790
onto one row of
your parent table,

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:09.110
you might run into limits of
read/write capacity and also

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:10.320
storage capacity.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.760
So be a little bit careful
there, especially also

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:15.397
for scalability reasons.

00:15:15.397 --> 00:15:17.480
A really, really cool
feature about Cloud Spanner,

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.130
which was really
important for us

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.530
when we adopted it
inside of Google,

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:24.546
was that we have no
downtime schema changes.

00:15:24.546 --> 00:15:26.420
So what you can do with
Google Cloud Spanner,

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.430
you can actually
alter your table,

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:32.420
add tables, add indexes
without any downtime.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:36.030
And I'm going to show you
that in a second as well.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.630
All right, so how
can you actually get

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.850
started with Google
Cloud Spanner?

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:44.410
So your first stop will be
cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:48.190
So this is our entry
page or the page

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:51.220
where you can go in and
learn about Cloud Spanner.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:53.740
You have links to the
docs, but you find also

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:55.760
a lot of the things that
I just talked about,

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:57.940
like comparison
between Cloud Spanner

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:01.720
and traditional databases
as well as NoSQL databases.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:04.900
We talk about use cases,
and you find all the way

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:11.170
to the bottom also some use
cases and studies around Google

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:12.260
Cloud Spanner.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:15.010
So I really can recommend
to read the Quizlet Blog.

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:17.230
If you're coming from a
charted MySQL database,

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:21.040
you want to know how
Quizlet did the D

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.020
charting into Cloud Spanner.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:26.170
This is a really good blog,
of course, very technical.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:31.960
All right, so you get started
in the Google Cloud Platform

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:32.770
console.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:34.643
So console.cloud.google.com.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:41.680
And there this is this home
scheme that you see here.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.480
Now, they have multiple
options to go to Cloud Spanner.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:47.260
You either use the
Search box in the top.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:50.710
You can actually go to the
Search bar by hitting slash,

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:53.290
as you might be
familiar from WIM.

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.040
But you can also use the
hamburger symbol here

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.140
in the left upper corner
and then go to Spanner.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.990
You find it's somewhere
in our drawer.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.180
You go to Spanner,
and there it is.

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.460
I have already one
instance created

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:08.240
I'm going to talk
about in a second.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:10.150
But let's create
an instance first.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:12.069
So you click on Create Instance.

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:15.180
You give it a name.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.680
You say cloud on air.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:20.170
It fills in the instance
ID automatically.

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:21.790
You can change it if you want.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:26.619
Then you have the
opportunity to choose

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:28.720
between a regional or
multi-regional instance.

00:17:28.720 --> 00:17:32.150
So regional is basically
a Cloud Spanner instance

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:35.070
distributed across three
zones in one region.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:37.300
Multi-regional gives
you the opportunity

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.430
to distribute your
Cloud Spanner instance

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:43.240
across multiple regions, up to
a global configuration where

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:49.660
you have the read/write
replicas in the US,

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:51.220
and then you have
read-only replicas,

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:55.090
for instance, in
Europe and in Asia.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:59.720
So we're going to choose
and use US-central1 here.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.520
You see also on the right
side what the US-central1

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:06.650
configuration is, like
all the API names,

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:09.210
replicas, how many
replicas you get.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:13.840
Our SLA for a regional instance,
it is four nines of SLA.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.300
And to be qualified
for the SLA, you

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:19.620
need to at least have
three nodes running.

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.990
So I'm going to do
three nodes here.

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:26.310
You're going to see what the
cost is per hour for the nodes

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:28.110
and then also the storage cost.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:31.410
You click Create, and
within seconds literally

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.370
you get your own instance.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:37.770
Now you can start and create
your database here and create

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:39.660
a schema and
everything, but I want

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:42.820
to show you how to do
this programmatically.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:48.110
So let's switch over to
my IntelliJ instance.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:50.160
And I'm going to show
you how to get started.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:55.500
So I choose for this session
to show you the Java client.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.510
So to get started
with the Java client,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:00.480
you have to either
use Gradle or Maven.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:03.360
In this case, I
use Maven POM file.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.450
And you basically select the
Google Cloud Spanner Client

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:12.270
Library for Java, currently
in version 0.34 0 beta.

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:15.600
You have to import also
to Guava Library and then

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.760
the Google authentication
to authenticate

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.950
against Cloud Spanner.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:24.250
So it's actually very
simple to get authenticated.

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:27.550
If you run your client
on a GC instance

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:30.610
or from your laptop, which
is set up with G-Cloud, which

00:19:30.610 --> 00:19:36.130
is our SDK, which comes
with a CLI and everything,

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.770
you can actually use the
authentication context

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:41.260
of either your GCE
instance or your laptop

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:43.070
that is already authenticated.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:46.060
So I'm authenticated against
my project with my laptop.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.430
And to use the
authentication there,

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:51.900
I just do google.credentia
l.getapplicationsdefault.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:52.930
One line.

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:53.430
All right.

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.810
I captured some exceptions
here, but basically it's

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:57.160
one line you authenticated.

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:58.430
Great.

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:02.560
So next thing that I need is I
need the instance in my project

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.780
that I want to talk to
and the database that I

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:05.740
want to talk to.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.280
So here in this case, I
talk about Spanner Cloud

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.900
and the instance
that I just created.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:17.150
And I'm going to create a
database with the name demo.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:20.070
So let's jump into
Create Database.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.130
So for the Create
Database, we basically

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:24.370
have to get a
database admin client.

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:26.250
So we get that from
our Spanner library.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.100
You get the database
admin client,

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.830
and we say, what
is the instance ID?

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:33.770
I filled that in
through this function,

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.082
and I want to also
know the database ID.

00:20:36.082 --> 00:20:37.790
So what you see here
is I actually create

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:42.320
a database in that instance with
the-- so in my Spanner Cloud

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:45.530
OnAir instance, with
the database name demo,

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:50.120
and then I define the
DDL here-- so the schema.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:53.210
And I recommend everybody,
like, if the first schema

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:54.740
that you create
with your database,

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:58.670
create it right away
with your database.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.850
If you create it after your
database, it will take a while.

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:06.080
Schema changes are not as
fast as queries and things

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:08.900
like that, so they can
take up to minutes.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.910
And depending on if
you create indexes

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:16.250
that need to be backfilled,
it can take hours or days.

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:19.130
So here in my case, I'm
creating an account table

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:20.560
with three columns--

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.220
account ID, name, and email--

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:24.190
and I create an address table.

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:26.030
And I interleave all of this.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.460
So with this interleaving,
I do the co-location,

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.860
and I also have that one
feature that actually

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:32.540
can do a delete cascade.

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:34.940
So if I delete the
parent row, I actually

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.710
can make sure that
all the addresses

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:41.820
in my address table for that
parent are deleted as well.

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.910
So let me run this.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:48.070
This should take only
a couple of seconds.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:50.990
Now, the set up of the client
takes a couple of seconds.

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:54.240
So this is not like
coming back right away,

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:57.000
but you see down here
that it actually finished.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.300
So we can go back and check
if our instance actually

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:01.060
was created.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.360
And we see we have
our demo database.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:04.890
All right, very cool.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:06.630
So now, with this
demo database, I

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.810
can do things like I
can see the schema.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.260
You see down here that
I show the schema.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.730
I see my indexes.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:15.800
I can run queries.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.880
I'm going to show in a second.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.270
And I can inspect these queries.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.110
See query plan, see
where is time spent,

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.550
optimize my queries,
things like that.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:28.560
I also have features in the
console where you can actually

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:34.350
directly add data in
the Cloud Console.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:37.260
So if you want to insert data,
edit data, or delete data,

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:40.830
you have the possibility to do
that directly from the console.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:44.070
Especially good if you do some
testing, things like that.

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:46.070
All right, so we have
created the database.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:51.062
So let's go back to
our Java program.

00:22:51.062 --> 00:22:52.770
And the first thing
that I'm going to do,

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:54.640
I insert some data.

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:56.000
So let's go to the Insert.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:01.266
What you can see here is we are
creating a list of mutations.

00:23:01.266 --> 00:23:04.860
So mutations are basically where
you group any kinds of changes

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:06.730
for the database together.

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:09.155
So in this case, I'm bolding
actually insert mutations,

00:23:09.155 --> 00:23:12.960
where I insert a bunch of
data into my account table

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.360
as well as in my address table.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.280
I want to point out one thing.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:23.890
I'm using here an ID which
I declared as string 32.

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:24.940
And that's nothing else.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:29.280
And I use UID version 4
and then convert it to hex.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:34.810
And by that, I basically
have a string of 32 byte.

00:23:34.810 --> 00:23:37.170
So why do I use UID?

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.820
In a distributed
database, you don't

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.950
want to use something
that is monotonically

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.290
increasing or decreasing.

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:46.470
The reason is that we
split the tables by ranges

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.500
by primary key, and if you have
something that is monotonically

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.590
increasing or decreasing, you
always end up in the first

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:55.470
or in the last bit, by that
overloading the node that

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:57.174
is responsible for that split.

00:23:57.174 --> 00:23:58.590
So if you want to
get the best out

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:01.170
of your instance, the
best out of Cloud Spanner,

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.390
you want to distribute your
load as best as possible.

00:24:04.390 --> 00:24:07.380
So that's why I'm using a
UID as a primary key here.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:10.630
So let's run this,
and we're going

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:16.290
to go back and see if this
data was actually created.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:19.520
So now in my address table, I
should be able to go to Data,

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:21.490
and you see that
I have some data.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:22.650
OK, great.

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:26.510
The next thing that I want to
do is I want to do a read-only.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:28.760
So in my read-only,
what I do is I create

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:31.310
actually read-only query.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:32.840
This doesn't create any logs.

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.810
In this case, I'm just
selecting the name and an email

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:40.040
from my account
data and check where

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:42.530
the country is in Germany.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:45.320
So let's see if I actually
have some data in there that

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:46.310
is from Germany.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:49.390
So let me run this.

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:51.630
Really, to point out
here what is important

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:54.060
is that this, like, you
declare a single use

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:55.280
read-only transaction.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:57.150
By that, we know that
this can be log free.

00:24:57.150 --> 00:24:59.550
So we don't need to
acquire any logs.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:03.600
As you can see down here, I
have two users in my database

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:07.170
that are living in Germany.

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:12.130
The next thing that I want to
show is read-write transaction.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:15.360
So in a read-write transaction,
this is a little bit more

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:16.240
complicated.

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:21.720
So in Spanner Client in the Java
Library Client, what we provide

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:25.950
is actually the rerunning of
transactions if they fail.

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:30.570
And we do this by providing
a runnable transaction,

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.091
runnable so to say.

00:25:32.091 --> 00:25:33.590
So what we do here
is we're creating

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:37.500
a read-write transaction, and we
provided a transaction runner.

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:40.960
And in this transaction
runner, I do my transaction.

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:44.130
So what I'm doing here
is I'm executing--

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:47.670
I'm running a select statement,
checking for all accounts that

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:51.240
have my email address,
and then updating

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:53.310
the name in that account.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:56.950
And then I bundle this together
and send it to the transaction.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:26:01.030
So if I run this, this
should change my data.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.700
We can have a look
at the account,

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:07.210
if there is anything
changed to spanner.guru.

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.910
And as you can see here,
we have the name change

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:11.200
to spanner.guru.

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:14.780
All right, so one
note to the mutations,

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:18.820
mutations are important to
know about because there's

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:20.590
a limit in Cloud Spanner.

00:26:20.590 --> 00:26:22.960
So Cloud Spanner has a
limit of 20,000 mutations

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:24.170
per transaction.

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:28.630
But what is a mutation in this
context of the 20,000 limit?

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:31.240
Mutation is actually
any change on a cell

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:32.950
level in Cloud Spanner.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:36.970
So as an example, if I have
a table with five columns,

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:39.110
and I insert a
row in this table,

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:40.790
then I have five mutations.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:43.870
Now, if I define an index
on that table, which

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:47.155
indices another row, or
let's say another two rows,

00:26:47.155 --> 00:26:50.080
and I insert a
row into my table,

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:52.540
then it's actually
eight mutations, right?

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:53.800
So be aware of that.

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:55.780
If you see the
limit of, like, you

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:59.810
reach 20,000 mutations
per transaction,

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:01.960
that this is not 20,000 rows.

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:07.060
It is actually rows multiplied
by how many columns in the row

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:10.710
plus indices that you have.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:12.460
Last but not least,
I'm going to clean up,

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.562
but I'm going to do that later.

00:27:14.562 --> 00:27:17.020
All right, one thing that I
wanted to show you really quick

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:20.200
is I have actually
example database here.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:22.570
And that example
database does not--

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:26.230
like, it's imitating like
a ticket sales platform.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:30.430
Imagine a global ticket
seller who sells tickets

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:34.660
to events all over the world.

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.835
We did this test with a database
of 80 terabytes of data.

00:27:37.835 --> 00:27:40.210
So something that wouldn't
fit in a traditional database.

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:42.730
And we did the test
also with transactions

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:45.490
to a limit that wouldn't fit
in a traditional database.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:48.520
Now, I have a little smaller one
here, but I want to show you,

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:51.700
this is a query to get
actually seat availability

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:55.660
of all events that are happening
today from 9:00 AM, when

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:57.690
this cloud session started--

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.070
the Cloud OnAir
session started--

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:02.320
to tomorrow at 5:00 AM.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.990
And then I'm looking for all
the events that are happening,

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:08.200
and I'm also checking how
many seats are available

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:11.960
and how many seats are available
in total and things like that.

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:15.880
So what is remarkable
about this create,

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:18.280
you don't have to think
about charting or anything.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:21.010
This is just a query
how you would see it

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:24.100
in a traditional database, no
specialties about charting,

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:25.780
or things like
that, distribution.

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:27.800
This is all handled for you.

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.040
And you can also, right
here in the console,

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.680
get an explanation of your
query, see the query plan,

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:35.850
see how many rows were scanned,
how much time was spent.

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:39.147
And you can optimize the
query with this information.

00:28:39.147 --> 00:28:40.480
So it's really, really powerful.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:45.400
So the last couple
of minutes, let

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:48.950
me talk a little bit about
use cases in these industries

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:50.910
and verticals.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:54.187
So where does Cloud Spanner
help meet business goals?

00:28:54.187 --> 00:28:55.770
There are three
things that are really

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:57.760
important to mention here.

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:59.820
So one thing, as we
saw, Cloud Spanner

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:02.940
uses traditional
tools in the sense

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:06.630
of what you are familiar with
from traditional databases.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:07.770
So it has SQL.

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:09.190
It has ACID transactions.

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:13.080
So you don't need this logic in
your application layer, things

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:13.580
like that.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:16.560
So you can actually tap
into existing resources.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:19.350
Developers that are familiar
with relational database,

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:23.430
they have a very easy
transition to Cloud Spanner.

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:26.410
Another thing is with Cloud
Spanner out of the box,

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:29.820
you get performance and
scale and high availability.

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:33.360
The way that Cloud Spanner is
set up, it is high available

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:37.030
and has disaster recovery
mechanisms built in.

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:38.550
You don't have to
worry about that.

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:40.590
All of this is done by us.

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:44.850
Up to five nines that
we provide for that.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.310
We have regional and
multiregional instances,

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:50.400
low latency
transactions, and reads.

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.970
And as mentioned,
simplified operation fully

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:55.890
managed for you,
a couple of clicks

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.560
and you're set up
with your own instance

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:02.220
that you can interact
with, where you can

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:06.140
write data and read data from.

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:09.630
So what are a couple
of business use cases

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.440
that you can look into?

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:14.760
We look at four
main categories here

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.320
or themes I want
to talk about here.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:20.340
So the first one is
transactional consistency

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:22.650
over a database that
is growing a lot.

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.600
So imagine you
have a supply chain

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.210
management or an
Ecommerce platform,

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:30.330
and you have a lot of data
that you put in your database.

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:33.750
So you outgrow basically
on the storage side

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:35.880
your traditional
database systems.

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:38.365
Now, what you can do is, we
talked about this charting

00:30:38.365 --> 00:30:41.730
or go NoSQL, but you lose
the transaction consistency

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:43.770
or have it really,
really difficult

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.960
and have to build it into
your application, which

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:48.100
is very outgrown.

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:51.390
A second one is scale
for reads and writes.

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:56.100
So imagine you have a telecom
or a billing gaming company that

00:30:56.100 --> 00:31:00.120
does all kind of actions that
are happening on your gaming

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:04.650
platform or, for instance,
for a telecom company,

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:07.530
like all kinds of calls
that are going on need to go

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.630
into a transactional system.

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:11.610
Now, usually where
you top out on

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:13.680
is like the reads and
writes that you can

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:16.540
do on your relational database.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:19.650
Spanner, the way it is set
up, scales horizontally

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:20.770
for your reads and writes.

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:23.340
So you get some
consistency and scale

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.430
for reads and writes for that.

00:31:25.430 --> 00:31:28.980
Another use case that we see,
or theme, is global data.

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:30.870
Just imagine you
have something where

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:33.450
you want to have the
data all over the world.

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:35.790
You are a global
company, and you

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:38.280
want to have access to your
data from all over the world.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.920
And again, you want to have
a consistent view on this.

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:42.750
Imagine, like
financial services,

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:46.460
like banks that are operating
globally or insurances

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:48.930
or global call centers
that need actually

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:52.440
a few which are consistent
all over the world.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:55.770
And the third one is
database consolidation,

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:57.630
just think like logistics
or manufacturing

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:00.000
where you have the
supply chain again,

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.290
and you want to consolidate the
data from multiple data sources

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:05.550
into one database.

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:08.170
Again, to have a strong
consistent view on this,

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:11.460
but also the ease
of use because you

00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:13.950
have only one system
that you have to manage

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:21.030
and to deal with for your
data and your applications.

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:23.850
I want to close out
with a little bit step

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:27.300
out and do an overview of
where Cloud Spanner fits in

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.790
in the Google Cloud
Database Portfolio.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.980
So I talked about Cloud
Spanner and all the goodies

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:33.660
that Cloud Spanner
comes with, but we

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:37.140
understand that there are other
use cases where other databases

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:38.730
are more suitable.

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:42.480
So if you look on the left, you
have our App Engine Memcache,

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:45.675
which is good for mobile
development and app

00:32:45.675 --> 00:32:47.160
dev, things like that.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:50.600
We have our relational where
talked about Cloud Spanner

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:54.350
just now, and we have Cloud SQL
which is a traditional database

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:57.450
system in the flavors
of MySQL or Postgres,

00:32:57.450 --> 00:32:59.490
where you can scale vertically.

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:02.850
It is managed, again, you
manage for you read replicas,

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:05.650
and we can also do
master-master replication.

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:07.860
This is good for
applications where

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:11.070
you are good with a
vertical scalable system,

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.960
and maybe you
transition or might

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:15.350
create a system which
was running before

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:17.220
under MySQL Postgres.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:20.110
We have also our
nonrelational flavors.

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:22.380
So you can look at
Cloud Datastore, which

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:25.350
is a document-based database.

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:27.210
It's very scalable.

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:30.180
Big companies that are
well known around the world

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:34.080
have built on Cloud Datastore
and have scaled from a couple

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:36.970
users to millions, hundreds
of millions of users,

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:38.680
on Cloud Datastore.

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:43.290
We have Cloud Bigtable,
which is backing our search.

00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:47.250
For very fast reads and writes,
you can use Cloud Bigtable.

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:49.950
It is a row-based database.

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:54.090
So you have transactions on row
level but not across row level.

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:58.330
And then we have Cloud Storage,
which is for our plop storage,

00:33:58.330 --> 00:33:58.830
so to say.

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:04.170
With Cloud Storage, we
have different tiers

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:06.720
based on how often
you access the data.

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:08.460
You can save some
money on there.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:09.929
Or this really
cool Cloud Storage

00:34:09.929 --> 00:34:11.670
is also backed by Cloud Spanner.

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:16.060
So if you do an LS on
Cloud Storage in the US

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:18.489
and in Europe, you
always get the same view.

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:21.780
So all data that you
commit to Cloud Storage,

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:24.870
be it a video file
or any other file,

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:27.179
you will get a consistent
view on the data

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:29.100
that is in Cloud Storage.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:32.960
And last but not least,
really, really amazing product

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:37.235
is our BigQuery product, which
is our OLAP, so Data Warehouse

00:34:37.235 --> 00:34:41.400
product, where you can load
terabytes and petabytes of data

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:46.290
into BigQuery and query it in
minutes or seconds or minutes

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:47.310
actually.

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:50.610
Very, very powerful, like
very powerful queries,

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:53.550
you have UDFs in BigQuery
that you can use.

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:54.929
So please try it out.

00:34:54.929 --> 00:34:56.909
If you want to do
any data analytics

00:34:56.909 --> 00:34:58.920
have a look at BigQuery.

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:00.750
And with this, I want to close.

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:05.210
For a second, we're going to
switch into the Q&amp;A session

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:06.760
after we regroup a little bit.

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:08.190
So please stay tuned.

00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:10.410
We will get back with the
questions that you asked.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:13.970
So we have on the right
side, you should see the Q&amp;A

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:17.250
platform, where you are able
to actually put questions in,

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:19.627
and we're going to answer a
couple of those in a minute.

00:35:19.627 --> 00:35:20.460
Thank you very much.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:27.480
All right.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:28.850
Hello, back.

00:35:28.850 --> 00:35:31.390
We got a couple of really cool
questions from the audience.

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:33.280
So I'm going to go through them.

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:34.870
You should see
them on the screen.

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:36.850
And I'm going to go
through them one by one.

00:35:36.850 --> 00:35:38.470
So the first
question that we have

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:41.460
is what's the difference
between MySQL Postgres

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:44.080
or other relational
databases and Spanner?

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:46.290
So if you look at
traditional databases,

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:48.760
a relational database
like MySQL, Postgres,

00:35:48.760 --> 00:35:51.280
or any of the big
vendor databases,

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.010
they are by default a
vertical scalable system.

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:56.480
Cloud Spanner, on
the other hand,

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.170
is a horizontally
scalable system

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:02.680
without giving any
of the nice criteria

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:04.570
up from a relational database.

00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:06.210
So we have strong consistency.

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:08.800
We have asset
transactions in Spanner.

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:11.080
But we are
horizontally scalable.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:14.560
So you can load terabytes,
hundreds of terabytes, of data

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:15.490
into our database.

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:19.960
You can have millions of reads
and writes with Cloud Spanner.

00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:23.620
So that is the main difference
from Cloud Spanner and MySQL.

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:26.440
The second question was,
is it possible to migrate

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:29.830
from current SQL systems
like MySQL to Cloud Spanner?

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:33.460
It is definitely possible to
migrate, but I have to say,

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:36.200
like this is not to lift
and shift migration.

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:39.760
So because Cloud Spanner
is a distributed system,

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:43.060
there are some
things to be aware of

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:46.180
and that need to
be considered when

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:48.980
migrating from a MySQL system.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:53.710
So one of the things is we are
currently not supporting UDF,

00:36:53.710 --> 00:36:57.520
so User Defined Functions,
triggers, or stored procedures

00:36:57.520 --> 00:36:58.750
on Cloud Spanner.

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:01.540
So if you use any
of this, you need

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:05.050
to re-implement these things
in an application layer.

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:07.895
But you get all the scalability,
and an ACID transaction

00:37:07.895 --> 00:37:10.150
on Cloud Spanner.

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:13.000
Other than that,
you can currently

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:16.900
migrate from SQL systems by
using our Client Libraries.

00:37:16.900 --> 00:37:19.240
So you export from
your existing database

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:22.240
and then create a
schema, modify the schema

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:25.150
to be suitable for the
distributed system,

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:29.260
and then load your data into
Cloud Spanner and run it there.

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:33.550
The third question was, why
not just use NoSQL database?

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:35.300
One thing, consistency.

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:39.220
So if you look at normal
traditional NoSQL databases,

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:41.920
then the first thing that
you give up is consistency.

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:46.730
Most of the NoSQL databases,
to achieve the scalability

00:37:46.730 --> 00:37:49.930
and the high availability,
they are giving up

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:50.884
strong consistency.

00:37:50.884 --> 00:37:52.925
So many of these systems
are eventual consistent,

00:37:52.925 --> 00:37:56.740
and what that means
is, as an example,

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:58.600
you have the World
Cup, and the World Cup

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:00.430
is published to a database.

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:03.280
And somebody goes onto
a website and checks

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:06.670
the score of the World Cup
and sees, OK, it has finished.

00:38:06.670 --> 00:38:09.070
And this country
won the World Cup.

00:38:09.070 --> 00:38:10.930
And then the friend
that is sitting next

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:13.690
to you, you tell them like, hey,
this country won the World Cup.

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:15.560
And the friend is
going to the website.

00:38:15.560 --> 00:38:19.570
But he's going to a different
replica of your NoSQL database,

00:38:19.570 --> 00:38:22.480
and for him, the
game hasn't finished.

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:26.000
So the data hasn't
replicated to all replicas.

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:29.470
And so two people can
see different results.

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.120
In Cloud Spanner,
this cannot happen.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:35.720
If the data has been committed,
all replicas have that data.

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:38.200
And so if any
requests are coming

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.870
in from all over
the world, everybody

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:43.930
will see exactly the same data.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:45.940
The fourth question
is, does Cloud Spanner

00:38:45.940 --> 00:38:46.960
have Client Libraries?

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:49.300
Yes, we do have
Client Libraries.

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:52.760
We have Client Libraries
for Node.js, Java, PHP,

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:54.640
Go, and Python.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.560
And we are working on
a lot of other ones.

00:38:56.560 --> 00:38:58.550
We have also C#.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:04.360
We even have the C# ORM
connector already available.

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:08.710
And the fifth question was,
does Cloud Spanner encrypt data?

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:12.280
So Cloud Spanner, all the
data in flight and at rest

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:14.350
is encrypted on Cloud Spanner.

00:39:14.350 --> 00:39:17.950
So if you load any of your
data in there at rest,

00:39:17.950 --> 00:39:20.240
all the data is encrypted.

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:23.780
Now, if you want to have
encryption on top of that,

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.280
you need to put this
encryption on top of that.

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:29.470
While we're talking
about encrypting data,

00:39:29.470 --> 00:39:31.580
I want to talk about
the permissions

00:39:31.580 --> 00:39:33.430
that you can set
on Cloud Spanner.

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:36.940
So we have identity and
access management system

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:38.350
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:39:38.350 --> 00:39:40.090
We call it IM.

00:39:40.090 --> 00:39:42.610
And you can actually
set IM roles up

00:39:42.610 --> 00:39:44.920
to the database
level, who is allowed

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.000
to access through
a database read,

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:50.170
write, and execute
queries, things like that.

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:50.930
All right.

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:53.976
See if you have some other--

00:39:53.976 --> 00:39:56.370
OK.

